<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Foreground layer of the adaptive launcher icon, on the standard 108x108dp canvas.
    Only the centre 72dp is guaranteed visible; everything outside it is safe-zone
    padding the launcher may mask or crop, so every drawn shape below sits inside
    x/y 18..90.

    ## This replaces a PLACEHOLDER, and the placeholder was also a brand-colour leak
    What was here was a purple #7C4DFF chevron, marked "replace with real branding
    later". Purple appears nowhere else in this app — the palette is cobalt-led with
    coral accents, sampled from the mascot (D58) — so the one piece of art every user
    sees before they ever open the app was the one piece drawn in a colour the brand
    does not contain.

    ## The mark: a scroll, stopped
    A down-chevron (the scroll — the gesture the whole app is about) meeting a solid
    horizontal bar (the block). It says what the app does in one shape, reads at 48dp,
    and is nothing like BrainPal's mark.

    Deliberately NOT the mascot. The mascot is full-colour raster art trimmed to the
    character (tools/mascot_import.py), and a launcher icon has to survive a circular
    mask, a squircle mask and a monochrome themed treatment — which detailed art with
    its own internal colours cannot do. The mascot leads the app; the mark leads the
    launcher. See ic_launcher_monochrome.xml, which is this same geometry and only
    works BECAUSE the geometry is this simple.

    ## Colours are literals here, and that is the ONE allowed exception
    Brand.kt is the single place a brand colour is written (D58), and Kotlin cannot be
    read from a vector drawable. Same exception themes.xml already takes for the window
    background, and handled the same way: cross-commented on both sides so a palette
    change cannot silently leave the icon behind.
      #D6E4F7 = Brand.COBALT_SOFT   (the scroll — recessive, it is what you were doing)
      #F08090 = Brand.CORAL         (the block — the accent, it is what we did about it)
    The background is a separate layer: values/ic_launcher_background.xml = Brand.INK.
-->
<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:width="108dp"
    android:height="108dp"
    android:viewportWidth="108"
    android:viewportHeight="108">

    <!--
        The scroll. Two strokes rather than a glyph so it stays crisp under the
        launcher's own scaling, with round caps/joins so it reads as a gesture rather
        than as an arrowhead. It stops well above the bar: that gap is what makes the
        two shapes read as "coming down onto" instead of as one connected symbol.
    -->
    <path
        android:pathData="M54,30 L54,56"
        android:strokeColor="#D6E4F7"
        android:strokeWidth="9"
        android:strokeLineCap="round" />
    <path
        android:pathData="M38,44 L54,60 L70,44"
        android:strokeColor="#D6E4F7"
        android:strokeWidth="9"
        android:strokeLineCap="round"
        android:strokeLineJoin="round" />

    <!--
        The block. Solid, full-width, coral — the only FILLED shape in the mark, so at
        launcher size the eye lands on the stop and not on the scroll. Rounded ends
        match the pill language the bubble and the buttons already use
        (Brand.RADIUS_PILL_DP).
    -->
    <path
        android:fillColor="#F08090"
        android:pathData="M33,71h42a6,6 0 0,1 0,12h-42a6,6 0 0,1 0,-12z" />

</vector>
